  <div class="prose">
    <h2 id="kinds">Sources</h2>
    <p>Every value comes from one of four sources. Pages and the API name them so:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Measured.</strong> Observed by providers.dev by sending requests. A measurement names the access method, the client the requests went through, the date and the raw result.</li>
      <li><span class="origin added">providers.dev</span> <strong>Stated by providers.dev.</strong> A correction of an imported entry, or a value of an entry providers.dev adds, each resting on the provider's own page, named with the date it was read.</li>
      <li><span class="origin upstream">models.dev</span> <strong>Imported.</strong> Copied from models.dev and not independently checked. Its date is the date of the import.</li>
      <li><span class="origin pi">pi.dev</span> <strong>pi's catalog.</strong> What the model catalog of the coding agent pi states for an entry, imported and not independently checked (see <a href="#pi">pi's catalog</a>). Its date is the date of the import of pi's catalog.</li>
      <li><strong>API price.</strong> The list price of the API entry of the same model, shown on a subscription entry and marked "API price". It is the only value an entry takes from another one (see <a href="#prices">Prices</a>).</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A page shows one value per quantity, chosen from these sources by one rule (see <a href="#safe-values">One value per quantity</a>). It does not say which source the value
      is from, and shows no date beside it. Where the sources that state a value do not agree, a warning sign beside the value opens what each of them states, with its date.
      The API keeps every source: the value, its source, and each source's own statement beside it.
    </p>
    <p>
      providers.dev takes no payment for listing, correcting or measuring an entry. A provider may supply documentation, or access for a measurement; the result is published
      whatever it shows. A provider can write to <a href="mailto:office@providers.dev">office@providers.dev</a>. How to propose a value, with the evidence it needs, is on the{" "}
      <a href="/docs/contributing/">contributing</a> page.
    </p>

    <h2 id="safe-values">One value per quantity</h2>
    <p>
      Five quantities have one value on every entry, its safe value: the context window, the maximum output, the short and the long cache lifetime, and the price. Each is worked
      out once, when the data is built, and published in the API (<code>providers_dev.safe</code> of an entry, <code>safe</code> of a summary) with what every source states
      beside it. The pages print it, compare nothing and work nothing out.
    </p>
    <p><strong>The order.</strong> The value is the statement of the first of these sources that states one:</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Measured</strong>: the most direct measurement of the entry (below).</li>
      <li><strong>providers.dev</strong>: a correction with its evidence, or the value of an entry providers.dev adds.</li>
      <li><strong>models.dev</strong>: the entry as imported.</li>
      <li><strong>pi.dev</strong>: pi's catalog, where it lists the entry.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      Where no source states one, the value is <em>Unknown</em>. A price is taken whole from one source, never mixed: a part one source lacks, a cache-read price say, is not
      filled in from another. providers.dev does not measure prices, so a price is providers.dev's, models.dev's or pi's.
    </p>
    <h3>Which measurement counts</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Every client a measurement goes through has a kind: plain requests sent straight to the provider, the vendor's own client (such as OpenAI's Codex CLI), or a proxy
        (Provimux, plain or with a coding agent on top).
      </li>
      <li>
        On an entry reached with an API key, only requests sent straight to the provider count. On a subscription login, the vendor's own client counts first; where it measured
        nothing, requests sent straight to the provider; where none of those either, a proxy. Measurements of the other kinds stay published on the entry's page, marked{" "}
        <em>Not counted</em>, and give no value.
      </li>
      <li>
        This is decided for each quantity, and for each cache lifetime on its own. Within the kind that counts, the lowest value counts, and the warning names the measurement
        it is: its client, its date and its raw result.
      </li>
      <li>
        A measured context window is the window the route names, or else the largest input it was seen to accept, below a refusal or read to the end. That one is a lower bound,
        shown as <em>at least</em> (≥ in the lists, rounded down, never up), and it is the value also where a later source states more. A measured maximum output is the lowest
        output limit a measurement read. A measured cache lifetime is, for each evaluation, the oldest age up to which every check hit, from the immediate re-send on (see{" "}
        <a href="#lifetimes">Cache lifetimes</a>).
      </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>What counts as a statement</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>A source that states nothing has no say: a field that is missing or <code>null</code>, and a limit of 0, which means none stated.</li>
      <li>A cache lifetime of 0 (the entry does not cache) and a listed price of 0 are statements. A lifetime of 0 is shown as <em>None</em>, whatever its source.</li>
      <li>pi writes 0 both for a price it does not charge and for one it has not recorded, so pi's 0 states nothing. pi states no price for a subscription entry.</li>
      <li>
        providers.dev can publish a value as unknown on purpose, where the imported one does not hold for an access method. That is its statement: the value is{" "}
        <em>Unknown</em>, no later source fills it, and the warning shows what the others state.
      </li>
      <li>A subscription entry's price is the API list price of the same model: the statements of providers.dev and models.dev are those of that API entry.</li>
    </ul>
    <h3>One refinement</h3>
    <p>
      A source states the context window either as a cap on the input alone or as a window that the input and the response share. Where the first statement is a window and a
      later source states a cap that is not above it, the cap is the value: a measured total is never published above a stated cap on the input inside it.
    </p>
    <h3>When sources disagree</h3>
    <p>A warning sign stands beside a value where two of the sources that state it contradict each other:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>two numbers, a maximum output, a lifetime, or two windows of the same kind, when they are not equal;</li>
      <li>a cap on the input and a window, when the cap is above the window (a cap inside the window is what a window allows);</li>
      <li>
        a measured context window and a stated one, when the route names a window below it or another window than a stated window, when an input at or below 99 % of it was
        refused, or when an input at or above 101 % of it was accepted. An input accepted below the stated value contradicts nothing: the walk stopped there. The 1 % margin is
        for sizes: a refused size is the probe's own count, and routes add tokens of their own;
      </li>
      <li>
        a measured lifetime and a stated one, when every check still hit after the stated lifetime, or when a check missed at an age the stated lifetime covers. One miss is
        enough: it already lowers the measured value;
      </li>
      <li>a value providers.dev publishes as unknown on purpose, and a value a later source states;</li>
      <li>
        two prices, part by part: the input, output, cache-read and cache-write prices and the tiers by size of request, when both sources state the part and the numbers are
        not equal; and the other rates of a price, between providers.dev and models.dev.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      A source that states nothing does not disagree, and prices agree only when exactly equal. The sign opens, for each source that states the value, what it states and its
      date; a measurement with its client and raw result, a correction with its evidence. The value on the page stays the first source's. On the price table the sign is on the
      row of each part that differs, and on the conditional rates for the tiers and the other rates; in the lists and the tiles, on the price as a whole. The sign opens with a
      click, a tap, or Enter, and closes with Escape. In the API a warning is <code>agreement: "differ"</code>, and <code>safe.differing</code> lists the quantities that carry
      one; the catalog's <em>Warning</em> filter is the parameter <code>disagree</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="unknown">Unknown values</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>A missing limit is <em>unknown</em>. It is not zero and not unlimited, and it never matches a minimum filter.</li>
      <li>A missing price is unknown, not free. A missing release date is unknown too, and such an entry never matches the "Released" filter.</li>
      <li>For caching, models.dev distinguishes <code>cache = false</code> (the entry does not cache) from no <code>cache</code> field (nothing is stated). Both are kept: "None" and "Unknown".</li>
      <li>A field can be published as explicitly unknown where the upstream value does not hold for an access method. The field is then omitted and the reason is recorded.</li>
      <li>
        Every entry page has the same sections, the same tables and the same rows, so that two entries can be read side by side. A row with no value is not left out. It says one of four
        things: <em>Unknown</em> (no source gives the value), <em>Unsupported</em> (a source says the entry cannot do it), <em>Not evaluated</em> (providers.dev has not measured it) or{" "}
        <em>Not applicable</em> (the field has no meaning for this entry). A subscription entry with no API price to refer to says <em>In plan</em>: there is no price per token.
        A list with nothing in it, such as the corrections of an entry, says <em>None</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        The API follows the same rule. Every object providers.dev publishes has all of its fields on every entry: an unknown value is <code>null</code>, never a missing field
        (<a href="/docs/api/#unknown">unknown values in the API</a>).
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="limits">Limits</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Context window</strong>: the most input one request can carry on this provider and access method, in tokens as the provider counts them, its safe value
        (<code>safe.input_window</code>; see <a href="#input-window">Context window</a>).
      </li>
      <li><strong>Maximum output</strong> (<code>safe.max_output</code>): the most one response can hold. It is not the default a provider applies when none is requested.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Usable context</strong>: the window minus the system prompt and tool definitions of the client in use. That overhead varies, so no usable figure is published. The context
        window is not that: it is what the route accepts, the client's own prompt and tools included, not what is left after them.
      </li>
      <li><strong>Compaction threshold</strong>: a setting of an agent or client. It is not a provider limit and is not published as one.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Limit evaluations</strong>: a limit evaluation sends requests of several input sizes on one access method. It records the largest input accepted, the smallest refused,
        and the window the route names where its answer names one; the entry page lists each evaluation with these numbers. A window listed by a client (a default the client
        works with) is not taken as the limit.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="input-window">Context window</h2>
    <p>
      On every page, <em>Context window</em> is the input window: the most input one request can carry on that provider and access method, in tokens as the provider counts
      them, the client's own system prompt and tool definitions included. It is the most a conversation can be. In the API its name stays <code>input_window</code>:{" "}
      <code>safe.input_window</code>, the same value in <code>providers_dev.limits.input_window</code> and <code>input_window</code> of a summary.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A source states it as <code>limit.input</code>, a cap on the input alone, or else as <code>limit.context</code>, a window the response is counted against too. A
        window written onto an added entry from a measurement is a measurement. Nothing is taken from another entry, not even from the API entry of the same model.
      </li>
      <li>
        Where the entry states a larger window that the response shares, a short note under the value says so ("1,050,000 including the response"): the API publishes that
        window as <code>safe.input_window.window_tokens</code>, and the note is there exactly where it does. <code>limit.context</code> and <code>limit.input</code> stay in the
        entry as imported and corrected, and the warning shows them where the sources disagree.
      </li>
      <li>A model's page gives the smallest and the largest context window among its entries, with ≥ where it is a lower bound, and the one of the lab's own entry.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A measured context window has limits:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>One account per route. Another plan, region or account can have another limit.</li>
      <li>Dated. A provider can change a limit the next day. A measurement goes stale as all providers.dev evidence does.</li>
      <li>
        A few requests. A walk costs quota for each request that is answered, so it stops early: the real limit lies between the accepted size and the refused one, and an{" "}
        <em>at least</em> value is a lower bound by as much as that gap. Where no refusal was seen, nothing shows where the limit is, only that it is not below the value.
      </li>
      <li>A refused size is the probe's count, not the provider's.</li>
      <li>
        Requests were short questions with filler text and a reply of a few tokens. A window that input and output share has less room for input when a long reply is asked for.
      </li>
      <li>A window a route names is its total. A cap on the input inside it shows only as a refusal.</li>
      <li>
        An input accepted with nothing refused, no window named and no check that the reply read it to the end gives no value: a route can answer a request without reading all
        of it.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="prices">Prices</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Prices are list prices in US dollars. The price of an entry is one source's, whole: providers.dev's where it corrects one, else models.dev's (<code>cost</code>), else
        pi's (see <a href="#safe-values">One value per quantity</a>). Every price is printed with every digit the source states.
      </li>
      <li>
        Text is priced per million tokens. Audio, images, video and web search are charges of their own, each in the unit the source gives: per million tokens or characters, per second
        or minute, per image or megapixel, and per 1,000 web searches. The entry page lists them under the text charges, and the catalog shows one where an entry has no text price.
      </li>
      <li>
        The catalog shows the base price of text input and output, and marks an entry that has conditional rates. Those are on the entry page, one block per condition with the price of each
        charge: a context size ("Context above 272,000 tokens"), a service tier, a region, a date from which a price changes, or the time of day.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Peak and off-peak hours.</strong> Some providers charge by the hour or the day of the week. The base price is the price outside the window. A rule that raises it in its
        window is marked <em>Peak hours</em>, one that lowers it <em>Off-peak hours</em>, with the window in UTC and the resulting prices.
      </li>
      <li>
        A conditional rate holds for the whole request that matches its condition: a request above a context threshold is priced at the higher rate as a whole, not only for the part
        above it. That is how models.dev defines its rules, and the order in which they apply: base prices, then its overrides in order, then every matching multiplier. Where a multiplier
        applies, the page shows the price it makes of each base price and the factor. It does not combine several conditions into one bill.
      </li>
      <li>A cache write price that differs by lifetime is shown with each lifetime.</li>
      <li>A price of 0 is shown as <code>$0</code>. It can mean free of charge, or paid for another way (a plan or a seat): the page does not decide.</li>
      <li>
        A subscription is not paid per token. So that a client can still work out what its usage is worth, every subscription entry shows the API list price of the same model, marked{" "}
        <em>API price</em>, and its page names the API entry the price is from. That is the entry a providers.dev entry was added from, or else the entry of the lab's own provider for the
        same canonical model. It is never what the subscription costs. Where the catalog has no API entry of that model, the entry says <em>In plan</em> and has no price, also where the
        source lists one for it: that figure stays in the entry's data as imported, and is not a price per token. Plan prices are not listed.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="access">Access methods</h2>
    <p>
      The same model reached with an API key and with a subscription can have different limits and cache behaviour. Each access method is a separate entry under its own provider, linked to the
      same model by <code>canonical_id</code>. An added entry takes only identity from the upstream entry it names
      ({IDENTITY_FIELDS.map((field, i) => <><code>{field}</code>{i < IDENTITY_FIELDS.length - 1 ? ", " : ""}</>)}). Limits, caching and capabilities are never copied across access
      methods: on an added entry a value appears only when there is evidence for that access method. The one exception is the price: a subscription is not paid per token, so a
      subscription entry shows the API list price of the same model (<code>providers_dev.pricing</code> has <code>billing: reference</code> and names the API entry in{" "}
      <code>reference_ref</code>), so that a client can work out what the usage is worth.
    </p>
    <p>
      Access is a classification: API key, subscription, or unclassified. Plans are not tracked: a plan's name appears only where a vendor's own model list attaches it to a model (see{" "}
      <a href="#listings">Model lists</a>), and plan prices, allowances and quotas never do. API prices are kept as imported. Where a service does not
      disclose which underlying model it serves, the entry has no <code>canonical_id</code>.
    </p>

    <h2 id="listings">Model lists</h2>
    <p>
      A provider's page has one table of its models: its entries, and the models the lists providers.dev has name for that provider and access method, evaluated or not. Its
      entries come first, with their values, prices and cache lifetimes. Under them, folded behind one line, are the models a model list names that have no entry: what the lists
      state of them, and no price or cache data. The lists are published as <a href="/api/v1/listings"><code>/api/v1/listings</code></a>. A listing joins the lists of four
      sources, each model once: the vendor's own list, read through a login by the daily discovery of <a href="https://github.com/ngvoicu/groundtruth">groundtruth</a> and published
      in its <code>listed.json</code>, with the client that read it named beside the list; a list providers.dev states, with evidence; the models models.dev lists for the provider;
      and the models pi's catalog lists for it. No list is taken as the whole: a login reads only the models its own subscription offers, and a plan tier or a subscription
      providers.dev does not hold can offer more, so the joined list too can miss a model. A model providers.dev leaves out of the provider with evidence is not on it, whichever
      list names it. The API names, per model, the lists that have it, and the page marks them in the model's sources, each with its list's date. An entry carries the mark of the
      vendor's own list where that list names it: <em>subscription</em> where it was read through a subscription login. Each value of a model without an entry (context window,
      output limit, release date) is from the first of the same sources that states it, and its hover text names that source. A list is not a set of entries: nothing of it is
      written into an entry's limits or into a safe value. The date of a list is the day it was read, or the day of the import; a list read by the discovery or stated by
      providers.dev is marked stale like other providers.dev evidence. Where the vendor's own list attaches plan names to a model, they are shown as it writes them; never prices,
      allowances or quotas.
    </p>

    <h2 id="merge">Merge rules</h2>
    <p>The published dataset is computed from three separate inputs on every build. Nothing is edited in place.</p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>The upstream snapshot</strong>: the last import of models.dev that passed validation.</li>
      <li><strong>Additions</strong>: providers and entries that models.dev does not have.</li>
      <li><strong>Corrections</strong>: single fields set on upstream entries. They take precedence.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>pi's catalog is a fourth input that sets no field of an entry and stands last: what it states is published under <code>providers_dev.pi</code>, and its limits, prices and lifetimes are the last source of the safe values (see <a href="#pi">pi's catalog</a>).</p>
    <p>A correction is a patch applied field by field:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll max-w-[62rem]">
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">In the correction</th><th scope="col">Published</th><th scope="col">Example</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th scope="row">Field not mentioned</th><td>The upstream value, which keeps following upstream</td><td>Setting <code>limit.context</code> leaves <code>limit.output</code> tracking models.dev</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">A value, including <code>false</code> and <code>0</code></th><td>The corrected value</td><td><code>temperature: false</code> is published as false, not dropped</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">An object</th><td>Merged key by key</td><td><code>{`cache: { explicit: { ttl: ["30m"] } }`}</code> keeps upstream's <code>max_breakpoints</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">An array</th><td>Replaces the upstream array</td><td><code>ttl: ["30m"]</code> over <code>["5m", "1h"]</code> gives <code>["30m"]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>null</code></th><td>Explicitly unknown: the field is omitted and marked unknown</td><td><code>limit.context: null</code> where the upstream figure does not apply to this access method</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>"$delete"</code></th><td>Removed: the field does not apply</td><td><code>limit.input: "$delete"</code></td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row"><code>exclude: true</code></th><td>The entry or provider is left out and listed as excluded</td><td>A provider that has shut down</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">A catalog-wide rule</th><td>Every earlier version of a model family is left out, at every provider, including those a later import brings. Listed once among the exclusions, with its reason, date and the number of entries it removed</td><td>Claude Opus versions before 5.5, an editorial choice of providers.dev</td></tr>
        <tr><th scope="row">A rule by release date</th><td>Every entry whose release date falls in a span is left out, at every provider and of every type. An entry with no release date, or a placeholder date, is kept. Listed once among the exclusions, with its reason, date and the number of entries it removed</td><td>Models released in 2025, an editorial choice of providers.dev</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <ul>
      <li>If models.dev changes a corrected field, the correction stays. The entry's provenance shows the new upstream value next to it.</li>
      <li>If a correction is removed, the current upstream value is published again at the next build.</li>
      <li>If models.dev comes to agree with a correction, the build reports it as redundant so it can be removed.</li>
      <li>If the entry a correction targets disappears upstream, the correction is not applied and no entry is recreated from it. It is listed under <a href="/evidence/#issues">open issues</a> and in <code>/api/v1/meta</code>.</li>
      <li>Every correction carries a reason and at least one source with a URL and a date. A build without them fails.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The same inputs always produce the same output, and the dataset version is a hash of that output.</p>

    <h2 id="sync">Daily import</h2>
    <p>
      Each day a scheduled job, at 04:17 and 06:47 UTC, fetches models.dev once per run, with a conditional request; a run that finds nothing new changes nothing. The import is validated before it replaces anything: it must be valid JSON in the expected structure, ids
      must match their keys, and it must not be empty or have lost a large share of its entries since the previous import. If validation fails or models.dev is unavailable, the previous
      snapshot stays in place and keeps being served; the footer and <code>/api/v1/meta</code> report the failed import. Visitors and API requests never trigger a fetch.
    </p>
    <p>
      The same run fetches pi's model catalog from <a href="https://pi.dev/api/models"><code>pi.dev/api/models</code></a>, automatically and on its own: it is checked in the same way, and a
      failure of either source leaves the other's import and its own last good snapshot as they were.
    </p>
    <p>The current import, source commit and counts are published at <a href="/api/v1/meta"><code>/api/v1/meta</code></a>, pi's under <code>pi</code>.</p>

    <h2 id="pi">pi's catalog</h2>
    <p>
      pi is a coding agent (<a href="https://github.com/earendil-works/pi">github.com/earendil-works/pi</a>, MIT License). Its model catalog, <a href="https://pi.dev/models">pi.dev/models</a>,
      says how pi calls each model it supports and at what price and limits it reckons. providers.dev imports it beside models.dev and is not affiliated with pi. A model that only pi
      lists is not an entry.
    </p>
    <p>
      pi is the last of the four sources of a value (see <a href="#safe-values">One value per quantity</a>). Its context window, maximum output, prices and cache lifetimes
      become the value of an entry only where no other source states one; there they are shown unmarked, like any other value, and the API names pi as the source. Where pi
      states a value otherwise than another source, the value's warning shows pi's figure beside the others'. pi's figures are never written into <code>limit</code>,{" "}
      <code>cost</code> or <code>capabilities</code>, and no value another source states changes with or without pi's data.
    </p>
    <p>
      A context window of pi's can be the window pi's own client works with rather than what the route accepts, and pi's price for a subscription entry is a list price too: pi
      states no price there. Where pi stands beside each field of the entry is published as well, in the entry's pi block (<code>providers_dev.pi.values</code>); the pages do not
      print it.
    </p>
    <h3>How pi calls the model</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        The section <em>How pi calls the model</em> of an entry's page holds what only pi returns, as pi states it: pi's name for the model, the request format in words and in pi's word,
        the address, what pi sends, whether pi treats the model as one that reasons, the thinking levels with the value pi sends for each (not the provider's list of effort values), pi's
        flags in three groups, and the request limits. None of it becomes a value of providers.dev's: pi's catalog names no source, and a flag speaks of one request format at pi's address.
      </li>
      <li>
        What pi does not state is <em>Not stated</em>, never "no": a flag pi does not name follows pi's own default, which is not published. A flag of the first group that is <code>true</code>
        or <code>false</code> is said as Accepted or Not accepted (Required or Not required for the one that names a requirement), with pi's name and value beside the
        words. A flag the table below does not name is printed as pi states it, under <em>Other flags</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        Where pi spells a provider or model id otherwise, providers.dev pairs the two by hand, and only where they are certainly the same; nothing is matched by similarity. The page
        then shows pi's own spelling.
      </li>
      <li>
        The page dates pi's data twice: when providers.dev fetched it, which moves only when pi's catalog changed, and when it was last checked and found unchanged. pi's values are
        imported, so they are never marked stale. When the last import failed, the page and the footer say so, and the last good catalog stays published.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>The page's words for pi's request formats and flags:</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll max-w-[62rem]">
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">Request format (<code>api</code>)</th><th scope="col">On the page</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {Object.entries(PI_FORMAT_WORDS).map(([api, words]) => <tr><th scope="row"><code>{api}</code></th><td>{words}</td></tr>)}
        <tr><th scope="row">Any other</th><td>pi's word alone</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="scroll max-w-[62rem] mt-4">
    <table class="facts">
      <thead><tr><th scope="col">pi's flag (<code>compat</code>)</th><th scope="col">Group</th><th scope="col">On the page</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {Object.entries(PI_ACCEPTS_FLAGS).map(([name, flag]) => <tr><th scope="row"><code>{name}</code></th><td class="whitespace-nowrap">{PI_FLAG_GROUPS.accepts.title}</td><td set:html={words(flag.label) + (flag.says === "accepted" ? ": Accepted or Not accepted" : flag.says === "required" ? ": Required or Not required" : ": the value as stated")} /></tr>)}
        {PI_OWN_FLAGS.map((name) => <tr><th scope="row"><code>{name}</code></th><td class="whitespace-nowrap">{PI_FLAG_GROUPS.own.title}</td><td>As pi states it: pi's own switch, not a statement about the provider</td></tr>)}
        <tr><th scope="row">Any other</th><td class="whitespace-nowrap">{PI_FLAG_GROUPS.other.title}</td><td>As pi states it</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">

    <h2 id="caching">Documented caching</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Automatic</strong> (<code>implicit</code> in the data): the provider caches the start of a prompt by itself. The request does nothing for it.</li>
      <li><strong>Manual</strong> (<code>explicit</code>): nothing is cached unless the request marks what to cache. The mark also picks the lifetime, where there is a choice.</li>
      <li>
        On an entry's page the <em>Cache modes</em> table has one row for each way of caching, supported or not, with its minimum prompt size, breakpoints and targets. The lifetimes the
        provider documents are statements of the two cache lifetimes (see <a href="#lifetimes">Cache lifetimes</a>), and stay in the API with their way of caching and the text
        as documented (<code>5m</code>, say). The request parameter and accepted values are recorded separately under Retention.
      </li>
      <li>An entry can have both: it caches by itself, and a request can also mark parts. Then each has its own line.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>The pages render <code>providers_dev.cache.documented</code>. An omitted mode remains unknown; only an explicit negative makes it unsupported.</p>

    <h2 id="evaluations">Cache evaluations</h2>
    <p>
      An evaluation sends a fresh prompt once so that it is cached, then sends it again at once and at each of several set ages, a few times per age. For each age the result records how
      many of those re-sends were made, how many were served from the cache, and that share in percent.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every evaluation made since 14 November 2026 follows the same ages: the prompt is sent again at once, then checked at <span data-meta="cache_ages_s"><code>cache_ages_s</code></span>,
      the later ages where the cache lasts. "At once" is the control: it shows that the prompt was cached at all and that the
      route reports cache hits, so that a later miss means the cache is gone and not that nothing was cached. Older evaluations keep the ages they were made with, and some start at 5 minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Results are shown per client. On an entry's page each lifetime in the <em>Cache evaluations</em> table is one client and one request setting, with its results by age, and
      opens onto each of its evaluations with their own results. The columns are the control and the ages above. A cell is the share of re-sends at exactly that age that were served
      from the cache, in percent rounded to a whole number, with the hits and checks under it; the hover text has the exact percentage. Nothing is rounded into a neighbouring age:
      7.5 minutes and 10 minutes are two cells. A cell with no check shows a dot; it is not a miss. A check made at an age that has no column (an older evaluation at 3 hours, say) is in
      the data at its exact age and is listed in words on the entry's page. The evidence page lists every evaluation, each with its percentages by age, rounded the same way.
    </p>
    <p>There are two retention profiles, because a request can ask for the cache in two ways, and the two answer different questions:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Short:</strong> the request asks for no lifetime, or for the shortest one the provider documents. This is the cache you get without asking.</li>
      <li>
        <strong>Long:</strong> the request asks for a longer lifetime, for example <code>cache_control.ttl=1h</code>, <code>prompt_cache_retention=24h</code> or <code>prompt_cache_options.ttl=12h</code>. Each measured lifetime
        shows the field and value its requests sent.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Both profiles are there on every entry. A profile with no lifetime measured says <em>No evaluation</em>; that is not 0 %, and it says nothing about whether the provider accepts a
      lifetime. What a request can ask for is a separate record, <em>Retention</em>, on every entry's page: whether a lifetime is accepted, the request field, the accepted values and which
      value gives which line. It is filled from the provider's own documentation, quoted with the record: the field as a request sends it (<code>cache_control.ttl</code>, say), its
      values, and the default. Where the short lifetime is what a request gets by leaving the field out, the record says so instead of naming a value. Sending a value, receiving a cache
      hit, or listing several documented lifetimes is not enough to call a field supported, and nothing is copied from one provider to another: a reseller of the same model stays{" "}
      <em>Unknown</em> until its own page says it. Requested settings stay visible in evaluations even when support is unknown. The results show observed behavior, not a guaranteed lifetime.
    </p>
    <p>
      The evaluations of one client with one request setting are added up by exact age; the results of different clients are never added together. The entry's page lists every
      evaluation, with its client, request settings, counts and raw result. An evaluation that checked no age and whose control hit shows that the prompt was cached and nothing about
      how long: it is listed under <em>Other evaluations</em> and is in no lifetime.
    </p>
    <p>
      Every number on these pages is in the API as published, counts, percentages and lifetimes alike; a page formats it and works out nothing of its own.
      See <a href="/docs/api/#cache">cache evaluations in the API</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each evaluation names the access method it was taken on and the client that sent the requests: straight to the provider, through{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/ngvoicu/provimux" rel="noopener">Provimux</a> (an open-source proxy, and for a subscription login often the only way in besides the
      vendor's own tool), or through the vendor's own command-line tool. Provimux is our distribution of{" "}
      <a href="https://github.com/router-for-me/CLIProxyAPI" rel="noopener">CLIProxyAPI</a>; evaluations before 22 November 2026 went through CLIProxyAPI 8.0.53.
      A result holds for that combination on that date. The evaluation source repository is listed in <a href="/api/v1/meta"><code>/api/v1/meta</code></a>, and every evaluation links to its raw result.
    </p>

    <h2 id="lifetimes">Cache lifetimes</h2>
    <p>
      Every entry has a cache lifetime for each retention profile, short and long: how long a cached prompt can be relied on. It is a safe value like the others (see{" "}
      <a href="#safe-values">One value per quantity</a>), from what was measured, what providers.dev and models.dev document, and what pi states, in that order.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A documented lifetime is the lowest the source documents for the profile. Where the provider documents that it does not cache, the lifetime is 0, shown as{" "}
        <em>None</em>.
      </li>
      <li>
        A measured lifetime is, for each evaluation, the oldest age up to which every check hit, from the immediate re-send on: one miss ends it, and an older age that hits
        again does not raise it. Within the kind of client that counts, the lowest across evaluations. A measured 0, also shown as <em>None</em>, means that a check already
        missed at the immediate re-send or at the youngest tested age; later ages may still have hit.
      </li>
      <li>Where no source states a lifetime it is not known and is shown as <em>Unknown</em>, never as 0.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      On an entry's page the <em>Prompt caching</em> table gives the two lifetimes. Under it, <em>Cache evaluations</em> lists, for each retention profile, the lifetime each
      client held with each request setting, with its checks and its results by age, those that count first; it opens onto each evaluation. Lifetimes are shown in minutes and
      hours (<em>5 min</em>, <em>24 h</em>); the exact seconds are in the hover text and in the API.
    </p>
    <p>
      A lifetime has limits. A measurement is a sample: a few requests from one account, on some days. An age between two tested ages is not known: a cache that hit at 30
      minutes and missed at 1 h 5 min may have ended at any point between. And a provider can change its cache behaviour without notice. The value is a published number made by
      this rule, not a guarantee.
    </p>

    <h2 id="stale">Dates and staleness</h2>
    <p>
      Every correction and evaluation has a date. Providers change cache behaviour without notice, so providers.dev evidence (a correction, an evaluation, a measured lifetime or input window)
      whose date is more than <span data-meta="stale_after_days"><code>stale_after_days</code></span> days before today is marked <span class="stale">stale</span>. It stays published, with its date.
    </p>
    <p>
      The number is <code>stale_after_days</code> of <a href="/api/v1/meta"><code>/api/v1/meta</code></a>, set once when the data is built. The API publishes the dates and this
      number; a page compares a published date with today's date using it, and that is the only thing a page works out. A reader of the API applies it to its own date.
    </p>
    <p>
      Imported values, models.dev's and pi's, are never marked stale: their date is the date of the import, given next to the source. A value without a warning shows no
      date; the date of each source's statement is in the warning and in the API.
    </p>
    <p>Licences and attribution are on the <a href="/docs/licensing/">licensing</a> page. How to propose a correction or a measurement is on the <a href="/docs/contributing/">contributing</a> page.</p>
  </div>
